 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Coming Soon Page Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a coming soon page?\", \"a\": \"A coming soon page is the page a site shows on its domain before the product or business behind it launches. It says what is coming and when, and it usually collects email addresses so the owner can tell people the day it goes live.\"}, {\"q\": \"How to set up a coming soon page?\", \"a\": \"Point your domain at one page that names the launch and its date, with a single email field. Store each signup in a table you own. With Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-landing-page-builder'>AI landing page builder</a>, you describe the page and Emergent builds it with the database behind it. The same coming soon landing page can sit on a subpath if the main site is already live.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a site coming soon page include?\", \"a\": \"Enough for a visitor to know they reached the right place and what happens next. Put the name of what is launching and a rough date at the top. Under it, one field for an email address. A contact address at the bottom helps press and partners, who will look for one before launch.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are good coming soon page examples?\", \"a\": \"Good coming soon page examples tell a visitor at a glance what is launching and what to do about it. A product drop pairs a countdown with one email field. A shop opening reads more like an <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-landing-page-builder/event-landing-page'>event landing page</a>, with an address, a date, and a reason to show up early.\"}, {\"q\": \"Where can I find coming soon page templates?\", \"a\": \"Website builders and design marketplaces both carry coming soon page templates, and many are free. Before picking one, check where the form sends the emails it collects, because a template covers the layout and not the list. Emergent skips the template step: describe the page, and the signup table comes with it.\"}, {\"q\": \"What goes in a coming soon web page template?\", \"a\": \"A coming soon web page template is usually one HTML file with a headline, a countdown script, a signup form, and social links. What it rarely has is a destination for the form. Connect that yourself, to an email tool or a database, before the page goes live.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is a website coming soon page template better than building your own?\", \"a\": \"Only when the page needs to look right and do nothing more. Building your own pays off once the page has to ask a second question after the email or greet returning visitors differently. On Emergent the free tier covers building and publishing that page at a live URL.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the difference between a pre launch landing page and a coming soon page?\", \"a\": \"A coming soon page holds the main domain until launch and keeps the message short. A pre launch landing page sells harder: it explains the product, answers doubts, and may take preorders. Run both when ads start before launch, with the paid traffic pointed at the longer page.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you share a coming soon landing page before launch?\", \"a\": \"Post one link wherever people already follow you: social bios, an email signature, the footer of a newsletter. Ask friends and early customers to pass it on. A thank you page after the form can carry that request, along with the link to copy.\"}, {\"q\": \"How long before launch should you make a coming soon page for your website?\", \"a\": \"As soon as the domain is yours and the launch has a month attached, even a rough one. Every week the page is up adds names to the list. A product with a long build can sit behind one for months, while a shop opening needs a few weeks to gather a crowd.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
